“When a deployment fails, first we detect the issue using monitoring alerts. 
We pause further rollouts to limit impact, analyze logs to confirm the root cause, and
immediately roll back to the last stable version using Kubernetes rollout undo or CI/CD redeployment. 
After rollback, we validate system stability and conduct a post-incident review to prevent recurrence.”
___________________________________________________________________________________________---
Kubernetes Rollback Procedure (With Commands)
Step 1: Check Deployment Status


First confirm the issue:

Using Kubernetes:

kubectl get pods
kubectl get deployments
kubectl describe pod <pod-name>
kubectl get events --sort-by=.metadata.creationTimestamp


👉 Look for: CrashLoopBackOff, ImagePull errors, failed readiness probes.

Step 2: Check Logs to Confirm Failure
kubectl logs <pod-name>
kubectl logs <pod-name> --previous


If multiple containers:

kubectl logs <pod-name> -c <container-name>


👉 This confirms if the issue came from the new deployment.

Step 3: Check Rollout History
4
kubectl rollout history deployment <deployment-name>


Check rollout status:

kubectl rollout status deployment <deployment-name>


👉 Identify last stable revision number.

Step 4: Pause the Broken Deployment
kubectl rollout pause deployment <deployment-name>


👉 Stops further rollout damage.

Step 5: Execute Rollback
4

Rollback to previous version:

kubectl rollout undo deployment <deployment-name>


Or rollback to a specific revision:

kubectl rollout undo deployment <deployment-name> --to-revision=<number>

Step 6: Verify Rollback Success
kubectl get pods
kubectl rollout status deployment <deployment-name>
kubectl get svc


Run health check:

kubectl port-forward svc/<service-name> 8080:80


👉 Confirm application is working.

Step 7: Resume Deployment
kubectl rollout resume deployment <deployment-name>
